      "trading_idea": "Zhang (2023) shows a *sparse*, sector-capped, non-negative tracking portfolio can reproduce a broad benchmark, and — crucially — that selection *frequency* ('number of holdings') is a more robust weighting scheme than coefficient magnitude ('market cap of holdings'). Intraday the same object exists: at any moment a handful of high-turnover 'hub' names carry almost all of the common factor because basket/ETF and institutional program flow lands on them first, so they are the sparse support of the market return; the remaining names are *tracked* rather than tracking, and receive the same factor impulse with a delay of seconds. The factor builds that sparse support by stability selection (non-negative, per-name-capped, per-sector-capped greedy replication of the cap-weighted market return, repeated on bootstrapped sub-windows, weighting names by inclusion frequency), forms the basket return, and trades the gap between each stock's basket-implied return and its realized return — long the names whose loading says they owe the market a move they have not yet made. Who is on the other side: liquidity providers in the slow, non-support names who widen after hub moves; the edge persists because re-hedging the periphery is capacity-constrained and disclosure/execution of the true crowded basket is stale (the paper's central friction). It dies if the gap is driven by genuine idiosyncratic news, in which case high-gap names should continue to diverge rather than converge, and if the signal survives only in the sparse support names themselves (the opposite of the transmission story).",
