      "factor_id": "patch_shape_basis_scale_context_gate",
      "name": "Patch-Shape Basis Decomposition with Scale & Volume Context Gate",
      "category": "microstructure",
      "trading_idea": "The time-series LLM paper's core engineering insight is that a path should be perceived as (i) non-overlapping normalized patches, (ii) a scale-free shape whose latent axes correspond to canonical characteristics (trend, level shift, oscillation, amplitude), and (iii) a magnitude context re-injected separately, with extra channels (multivariate signals) fed as tagged parallel streams. Applied to 10-second bars, this says the *same* recent price excursion has opposite forward implications depending on which shape axis carries it and whether the amplitude/volume context corroborates it: a genuine level-shift (step) patch accompanied by a coincident volume patch spike is informed repricing and persists over the next minute, whereas the identical step with flat volume is a liquidity/queue-depletion artefact and mean-reverts, and the high-frequency alternating (Nyquist) component is almost pure quote-bounce noise that always reverts. Market makers and short-horizon liquidity providers are on the other side: they must post continuously and cannot condition on the shape/volume interaction fast enough across the whole cross-section, so the mis-attribution of shape to information persists. It dies if step components revert regardless of volume confirmation, or if the alternating component shows continuation.",
