      "factor_id": "vl_granger_dtw_backlog_catchup",
      "name": "Variable-Lag DTW Alignment Backlog Catch-Up",
      "category": "statistical_arbitrage",
      "trading_idea": "Amornbunchornvej et al. show that follower series track leaders with *time-varying* lags, so fixed-lag cross-correlation/Granger tests miss real lead-lag structure; the DTW warping path itself carries the two objects we care about: the direction of emulation (average sign of the warp offsets, s(P)) and the *current* lag at the endpoint of the window. If a stock's intraday price path is a low-distortion, positively-lagged emulation of its sector's volume-weighted path, then at time t the stock is still 'replaying' the leader's state from t-\u0394_t, and the leader's move over (t-\u0394_t, t] is a mechanically identifiable, not-yet-emulated backlog that the follower should traverse next. The other side of the trade is slow-reacting liquidity in the follower name (thin queues, index/basket flow hitting the liquid leaders first), and the edge persists because measuring elastic (not fixed) lag requires path alignment that standard lead-lag screens do not run; it would be falsified if backlog-scaled forecasts show no incremental IC over a plain fixed-lag sector-beta residual or if the sign of s(P) carries no information about which side leads.",
      "description": "For each name, over a rolling window (~180 bars) build the stock's cumulative log-price path and the volume-weighted cumulative log path of its sector peers (stock excluded), both scaled by window volatility, then run band-limited DTW (Sakoe-Chiba width \u03b4max \u2248 12 bars) to get the warping path; retain (i) the emulation-direction score s(P) = mean sign of warp offsets, (ii) the endpoint lag \u0394_T, (iii) the emulation gain \u03b2 from regressing follower increments on the warp-reconstructed leader increments, and (iv) a variable-lag-vs-fixed-lag residual-variance gate (VL residual must beat both own-past and best fixed-lag residual). The signal is \u03b2 \u00d7 (leader cumulative return from t-\u0394_T to t), gated to names with s(P) > 0, sufficient alignment similarity, and a passing VL gate, then cross-sectionally demeaned/ranked within sector.",
      "prediction_horizon": 6,
      "suggested_horizons": [6],
      "source_paper_ids": ["variable_lag_granger_causality_for_time_series_analysis"]
    }
  ]
}

