      "factor_id": "second_order_peer_graph_flow_event_clock_damping",
      "name": "Second-Order Peer-Graph Flow with Velocity Damping on an Event Clock",
      "category": "statistical_arbitrage",
      "trading_idea": "The GDE paper's decisive result is that a *second-order* graph ODE (state = position AND velocity, with a radius-activated, time-varying adjacency and a viscoelastic spring-drag interaction) extrapolates a networked system far better than either a first-order graph flow or a graph-blind ODE. Mapped to equities: a name's short-horizon relative price is a particle whose neighbours are same-sector peers that are *currently close* in normalised price-state space (the radius gate), and the force pulling it back has two components — an elastic term in the peer-relative dislocation level and a damping term in the peer-relative dislocation *velocity*. Relative-value market makers quote and hedge off peers that are presently co-moving, so a name separating fast from a tight peer cluster is absorbing liquidity-demanding flow that gets damped within seconds; the level term alone is the over-mined pairs-reversal, while the velocity-Laplacian (damping) term is the under-exploited conditioning. Following the paper's irregular-timestamp handling, the ODE is integrated over each name's own *event clock* (fraction of recent bars with actual trading), so illiquid, rarely-traded names get a shorter effective integration interval and a proportionally smaller forecast displacement. Falsifiable: if setting the damping coefficient beta to zero (or removing the radius gate so all sector peers are neighbours) leaves the IC unchanged, the mechanism is just repackaged peer reversal and the idea dies.",
