      "name": "GP Covariance-Break Persistence Flip (idiosyncratic GLRT gate)",
      "category": "statistical_arbitrage",
      "trading_idea": "Han et al. show that fitting one stationary kernel across a covariance structural break badly mis-states the smoothness (length-scale) of a path, and that a likelihood-ratio test on block-diagonal vs full covariance detects the break long before a Bayesian run-length filter does. At the 10-second scale the return autocovariance IS the microstructure regime: a high-persistence regime (long length-scale, positive lag-1 autocorrelation of mid returns) means order flow is being worked through the book and recent drift extends, whereas a low/negative-persistence regime means quote bouncing and inventory-driven noise, so the same recent move reverts. Most participants estimate one persistence parameter over a fixed lookback and therefore keep trading continuation just after the path has switched to choppy (and vice versa); the mispricing persists because re-estimating persistence in real time is a modelling cost, not an arbitrage capital cost. Falsifier: if the sign of (post-break minus pre-break) lag-1 persistence carries no interaction with the sign of the recent move, or if the effect is identical for market-wide breaks as for idiosyncratic ones, the mechanism is dead.",
      "description": "Per name, over a rolling window of 10s log returns split at its midpoint, fit AR(1)/exponential GP kernels (Yule-Walker) to the whole window and to each half, form the exact closed-form Toeplitz GLRT statistic 2L = r'Sigma^-1 r - r'Sigma_block^-1 r + ln(|Sigma|/|Sigma_block|), z-score it against its own trailing distribution and subtract the cross-sectional median (isolating idiosyncratic covariance breaks); the factor is this break confidence times tanh of the pre-to-post change in estimated persistence times the standardized post-break cumulative return, cross-sectionally demeaned — i.e. continuation when the path just became smoother, reversal when it just became choppier.",
      "prediction_horizon": 6,
