      "name": "GARCH-Filtered Magnitude Long-Memory Continuation/Reversal Switch",
      "category": "volatility",
      "trading_idea": "Oh, Kim & Eom show that at high frequency the long memory of the |return| (magnitude) series is only partially explained by GARCH-type conditional heteroskedasticity: after GARCH(1,1) filtering, daily volatility memory collapses to H~0.5 but intraday volatility memory survives at H~0.6-0.65. That surviving memory is the footprint of genuinely endogenous, self-exciting activity (order-splitting, herding, slow information diffusion) rather than a mechanically decaying variance level, and it is exactly the state in which a signed price impulse gets pushed further in the same direction by the follow-on flow. Conversely, when the standardized-residual magnitude series is memoryless (H<=0.5, i.e. the local variance model already accounts for the burst), the impulse looks like an isolated liquidity shock and the usual 10-second reversal dominates. So the sign series (unpredictable on its own, per the paper) becomes tradable once conditioned on residual magnitude memory: the switch predicts whether the last minute's move continues or snaps back. Falsification: if the cross-sectional sign of forward 6-bar returns given a recent impulse is invariant to the residual DFA exponent, the mechanism is dead; the persistence story requires market makers who cannot tell endogenous from exogenous bursts and therefore mis-price continuation risk.",
