      "trading_idea": "Billings & Del Barco show that a Labouchère (negative-progression) staker enjoys long stretches of smooth wins and then dies in one burst, because the required stake grows with the unrecovered-loss ledger while capital is finite — ruin arrives when stake > bankroll. Intraday dip-buyers and inventory-averaging market makers behave exactly like Labouchère players: they fade each move, add size after each adverse tick, and their 'sequence' of unrecovered losses grows during a persistent trend. The falsifiable claim is that when the *implied next stake* implied by the loss ledger exceeds the *capital capacity* implied by current liquidity and volatility, these players stop absorbing and capitulate, so price continues in the trend (loss-streak) direction over the next ~1 minute rather than reverting. Counterparty: leveraged mean-reverters and passive liquidity providers whose margin/inventory limits are mechanical; the edge persists because their constraint binds fastest exactly when depth thins and vol spikes, and it would be killed if high ruin-proximity names instead reverted over the horizon.",
